1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,533
No crime. No charge.

2
00:00:02,133 --> 00:00:07,138
You kept every deposit under ten thousand
dollars — and the government took your

3
00:00:07,138 --> 00:00:08,077
account anyway.

4
00:00:10,577 --> 00:00:15,668
For thirty-eight years, a small cash
restaurant sat off the lakes country of

5
00:00:15,668 --> 00:00:17,543
northwest Iowa. Mrs. Lady's.

6
00:00:18,143 --> 00:00:23,048
A woman in her late sixties ran it six
days a week — she worked the kitchen, rang

7
00:00:23,048 --> 00:00:27,954
the register, and carried the day's money
to the bank herself. She was not hiding

8
00:00:27,954 --> 00:00:28,499
anything.

9
00:00:29,099 --> 00:00:32,861
Every dollar she deposited came from
plates she had sold that day.

10
00:00:33,461 --> 00:00:37,699
What the government did to her should
worry anyone who has ever run a small

11
00:00:37,699 --> 00:00:42,277
business on cash, because by the end you
will see how easily the same thing could

12
00:00:42,277 --> 00:00:42,842
reach you.

13
00:00:43,442 --> 00:00:46,971
There is a banking rule almost nobody
bothers to explain.

14
00:00:47,571 --> 00:00:52,473
If you deposit more than ten thousand
dollars in cash, your bank files a single

15
00:00:52,473 --> 00:00:55,141
report to the federal government. One
form.

16
00:00:55,741 --> 00:01:00,524
That is the entire obligation, and it
falls on the bank, not on you.

17
00:01:01,124 --> 00:01:05,474
The rule came out of a nineteen-seventy
law written to track organized crime and

18
00:01:05,474 --> 00:01:09,823
the dirty money moving through American
banks — cash so tainted that someone had

19
00:01:09,823 --> 00:01:11,944
to launder it before it could be spent.

20
00:01:12,544 --> 00:01:15,423
It was never aimed at a cash register in a
diner.

21
00:01:16,023 --> 00:01:19,552
The offense that trapped her was not even
part of that original law.

22
00:01:20,152 --> 00:01:24,558
It was added sixteen years later, in
nineteen eighty-six, when Congress made it

23
00:01:24,558 --> 00:01:28,908
a separate crime to deliberately split
your cash into smaller deposits to keep

24
00:01:28,908 --> 00:01:32,366
them under the ten-thousand-dollar line
and dodge that report.

25
00:01:32,966 --> 00:01:36,542
The aim was to stop launderers from
tiptoeing under the threshold.

26
00:01:37,142 --> 00:01:41,819
What it produced, decades later, was a
rule that could be pointed at anyone whose

27
00:01:41,819 --> 00:01:43,551
deposits happened to be small.

28
00:01:44,151 --> 00:01:46,752
That new crime has a name. Structuring.

29
00:01:47,352 --> 00:01:49,953
And the name inverts what you think a
crime is.

30
00:01:50,553 --> 00:01:55,117
The law does not only watch the large
deposits; it watches you for staying

31
00:01:55,117 --> 00:02:00,052
small, and it decides, on its own, what a
pattern of small deposits means — long

32
00:02:00,052 --> 00:02:02,581
before it asks you a single question, and

33
00:02:03,181 --> 00:02:06,850
long before anyone thinks to ask whether
it should be watching you at all.

34
00:02:07,450 --> 00:02:11,383
That last question — whether the
government should be watching an ordinary

35
00:02:11,383 --> 00:02:15,741
cash business in the first place — is one
almost no one in power stops to ask, and

36
00:02:15,741 --> 00:02:17,760
it hangs over everything that follows.

37
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:22,856
That rule asks something strange of an
ordinary person: to stay on the right side

38
00:02:22,856 --> 00:02:27,353
of it, you would first have to know the
reporting threshold exists, and then know

39
00:02:27,353 --> 00:02:30,295
that trying to stay beneath it is itself
the offense.

40
00:02:30,895 --> 00:02:34,099
Most people who run a small cash business
know neither.

41
00:02:34,699 --> 00:02:39,003
They only know that a stack of cash needs
to get to the bank, and that some weeks

42
00:02:39,003 --> 00:02:40,225
are busier than others.

43
00:02:40,825 --> 00:02:46,000
To an investigator reading a bank printout
months later, that ordinary rhythm — a

44
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,303
good Saturday, a slow Tuesday, a deposit
here, a smaller one there — can be made to

45
00:02:51,303 --> 00:02:52,389
look like a plan.

46
00:02:52,989 --> 00:02:57,503
And once it looks like a plan, the burden
of proving that it was not lands on the

47
00:02:57,503 --> 00:02:59,119
person who made the deposits.

48
00:02:59,719 --> 00:03:02,830
She had kept her deposits under ten
thousand for years.

49
00:03:03,430 --> 00:03:07,917
By her own account, the habit came from
her mother, who had once kept the books,

50
00:03:07,917 --> 00:03:12,068
and who told her to stay under the line
and skip the extra bank paperwork.

51
00:03:12,668 --> 00:03:16,476
She has said she never knew banks reported
large deposits at all.

52
00:03:17,076 --> 00:03:21,328
Whether anyone in the government would
believe that was, it turned out, entirely

53
00:03:21,328 --> 00:03:22,231
out of her hands.

54
00:03:22,831 --> 00:03:26,360
For most of those thirty-eight years, none
of this touched her.

55
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:31,804
The reports, the thresholds, the statute
with its section number, all of it ran

56
00:03:31,804 --> 00:03:34,808
quietly behind every cash deposit in the
country.

57
00:03:35,408 --> 00:03:39,030
She made food, she took in cash, she
banked it.

58
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:41,952
The law was watching the entire time.

59
00:03:42,552 --> 00:03:45,524
She simply had no idea it had started
watching her.

60
00:03:48,024 --> 00:03:51,961
In two thousand thirteen, without a
warning she could see coming, the

61
00:03:51,961 --> 00:03:56,012
government emptied the restaurant's
checking account. Every cent of it.

62
00:03:56,612 --> 00:04:01,554
Thirty-two thousand, eight hundred twenty
dollars and fifty-six cents, gone in a

63
00:04:01,554 --> 00:04:02,789
single legal stroke.

64
00:04:03,389 --> 00:04:05,432
Picture your own account for a moment.

65
00:04:06,032 --> 00:04:11,925
You go to pay a supplier, and the balance
is not low. It is not overdrawn. It is

66
00:04:11,925 --> 00:04:12,441
seized.

67
00:04:13,041 --> 00:04:15,084
She was never charged with a crime.

68
00:04:15,684 --> 00:04:21,586
Not money laundering, not tax evasion, no
allegation that she had done anything with

69
00:04:21,586 --> 00:04:26,644
the money except earn it, one plate at a
time. And the bills still came.

70
00:04:27,244 --> 00:04:29,241
The food still had to be bought.

71
00:04:29,841 --> 00:04:33,690
Her savings were locked inside a legal
case with a name that tells you almost

72
00:04:33,690 --> 00:04:35,089
everything you need to know.

73
00:04:35,689 --> 00:04:39,311
The case was not called The United States
versus Carole Hinders.

74
00:04:39,911 --> 00:04:44,692
It was called The United States versus
thirty-two thousand, eight hundred twenty

75
00:04:44,692 --> 00:04:46,366
dollars and fifty-six cents.

76
00:04:46,966 --> 00:04:50,774
That bears repeating, because it is the
strangest sentence in this story.

77
00:04:51,374 --> 00:04:55,832
The government was not prosecuting her. It
was prosecuting her money.

78
00:04:56,432 --> 00:05:01,208
This is civil forfeiture, and it runs
backwards from everything you assume about

79
00:05:01,208 --> 00:05:02,283
how the law works.

80
00:05:02,883 --> 00:05:07,657
In a criminal court, the government has to
prove you guilty, and you are presumed

81
00:05:07,657 --> 00:05:09,013
innocent until it does.

82
00:05:09,613 --> 00:05:14,327
Here, the money itself was treated as
guilty, and it was on her to prove that it

83
00:05:14,327 --> 00:05:15,093
was innocent.

84
00:05:15,693 --> 00:05:20,195
It is an inversion of everything the law
is supposed to do, and it is where the

85
00:05:20,195 --> 00:05:24,470
whole abuse lives: the government does not
have to convict you of anything.

86
00:05:25,070 --> 00:05:29,910
It only has to hold your money — and wait,
while you spend what you have left, at

87
00:05:29,910 --> 00:05:33,197
your own expense, to claim back what was
already yours.

88
00:05:33,797 --> 00:05:36,908
The thing it put on trial was a number in
a bank ledger.

89
00:05:37,508 --> 00:05:42,240
A number cannot testify, and it cannot be
found not guilty by a jury; it can only be

90
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:46,859
claimed back by an owner willing to spend
more than it might be worth to fight for

91
00:05:46,859 --> 00:05:47,028
it.

92
00:05:47,628 --> 00:05:49,439
The trap has a simple shape.

93
00:05:50,039 --> 00:05:53,568
To fight, you need a lawyer. A lawyer
costs money.

94
00:05:54,168 --> 00:05:56,629
Your money is the thing the government is
holding.

95
00:05:57,229 --> 00:06:01,690
So defending yourself means paying, out of
whatever you have left, to argue about

96
00:06:01,690 --> 00:06:03,452
savings you can no longer touch.

97
00:06:04,052 --> 00:06:08,671
Plenty of people in that exact position do
the arithmetic and walk away — they let

98
00:06:08,671 --> 00:06:13,065
the government keep the cash, because
fighting for it costs more than the cash

99
00:06:13,065 --> 00:06:13,572
is worth.

100
00:06:14,172 --> 00:06:18,067
Every time that happens, the seizure
becomes permanent without a judge ever

101
00:06:18,067 --> 00:06:19,884
ruling on whether a crime occurred.

102
00:06:20,484 --> 00:06:22,249
It does not need a conviction.

103
00:06:22,849 --> 00:06:24,799
It does not even need a charge.

104
00:06:25,399 --> 00:06:27,117
It only needs you to give up.

105
00:06:27,717 --> 00:06:30,225
And she was not the only one it was built
for.

106
00:06:30,825 --> 00:06:35,416
In the years around her case, a government
watchdog inside the Treasury would go

107
00:06:35,416 --> 00:06:39,548
back and pull the files on these
structuring seizures, hundreds of them,

108
00:06:39,548 --> 00:06:43,622
and what it found would turn one
grandmother's misfortune into a number

109
00:06:43,622 --> 00:06:46,893
that indicted the entire practice. That
number is coming.

110
00:06:47,493 --> 00:06:49,258
For now, back in Iowa.

111
00:06:49,858 --> 00:06:54,952
Over roughly a year of her deposits,
investigators had gone through her banking

112
00:06:54,952 --> 00:06:59,982
and decided the pattern itself was the
offense. No theft. No drugs. No victim.

113
00:07:00,582 --> 00:07:06,775
A small-town cook who, they said, made her
deposits too small, too often. That was

114
00:07:06,775 --> 00:07:07,455
the case.

115
00:07:08,055 --> 00:07:13,167
The thirty-eight years, the sales rung up
one plate at a time, none of it entered

116
00:07:13,167 --> 00:07:17,900
into it, because in a case against money,
the person barely appears at all.

117
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:22,993
For months, that is roughly where she
stood — her name attached to a case that

118
00:07:22,993 --> 00:07:27,717
did not even bear her name, her savings
frozen inside it, and no exit that did not

119
00:07:27,717 --> 00:07:29,042
cost more than she had.

120
00:07:29,642 --> 00:07:33,125
She was not the only person in the country
in that exact position.

121
00:07:33,725 --> 00:07:36,465
She would turn out to be the one whose
story got out.

122
00:07:38,965 --> 00:07:40,126
Someone noticed.

123
00:07:40,726 --> 00:07:45,566
On the twenty-fifth of October, two
thousand fourteen, The New York Times put

124
00:07:45,566 --> 00:07:46,949
her on its front page.

125
00:07:47,549 --> 00:07:52,054
The headline did the work by itself: "Law
Lets I.R.S.

126
00:07:52,654 --> 00:07:56,960
Seize Accounts on Suspicion, No Crime
Required." Overnight, the country was

127
00:07:56,960 --> 00:08:01,439
reading about a statute built to catch
money launderers, turned on a woman who

128
00:08:01,439 --> 00:08:04,310
sold Mexican food out of a small building
in Iowa.

129
00:08:04,910 --> 00:08:08,866
The Institute for Justice, a
public-interest law firm that fights

130
00:08:08,866 --> 00:08:12,944
exactly this kind of case, took her on and
demanded the money back.

131
00:08:13,544 --> 00:08:18,559
And the case that had moved so confidently
against her began, very suddenly, to give

132
00:08:18,559 --> 00:08:18,977
ground.

133
00:08:19,577 --> 00:08:24,196
Around the same time the pressure was
building, the tax agency had signaled a

134
00:08:24,196 --> 00:08:28,995
change of its own: going forward, it would
generally stop seizing money in cases

135
00:08:28,995 --> 00:08:32,534
like hers, where the cash came from a
plainly legal source.

136
00:08:33,134 --> 00:08:38,150
About three months after her lawyers
stepped in, the government moved to

137
00:08:38,150 --> 00:08:43,862
dismiss the case and return every dollar
it had taken. She won. On paper, she won.

138
00:08:44,462 --> 00:08:46,830
But the fine print is where this turns.

139
00:08:47,430 --> 00:08:50,959
The government did not dismiss the case by
admitting it was wrong.

140
00:08:51,559 --> 00:08:57,085
It dismissed the case "without prejudice."
In plain terms, that means it walked away

141
00:08:57,085 --> 00:09:02,612
without conceding a thing, and it kept the
right to come back. It did not apologize.

142
00:09:03,212 --> 00:09:05,209
It did not say she was innocent.

143
00:09:05,809 --> 00:09:10,684
It simply let go, and left one question
hanging over her: had she actually beaten

144
00:09:10,684 --> 00:09:15,500
them, or had they just decided she was
more trouble than her thirty-two thousand

145
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:16,583
dollars was worth?

146
00:09:17,183 --> 00:09:21,781
Those two words, without prejudice, come
back later, and they cost her.

147
00:09:22,381 --> 00:09:25,492
For a moment, it looked like the system
had corrected itself.

148
00:09:26,092 --> 00:09:30,736
A newspaper shone a light, a policy
changed, the money came home.

149
00:09:31,336 --> 00:09:33,658
But what it took to get there is telling.

150
00:09:34,258 --> 00:09:39,748
Not a courtroom, not a ruling that she was
innocent, a reporter, a front page, and a

151
00:09:39,748 --> 00:09:44,846
national audience, a run of luck that
almost nobody in her position ever gets.

152
00:09:45,446 --> 00:09:49,781
For every account that made the front of
the Times, there were others drained

153
00:09:49,781 --> 00:09:54,341
without a word, owned by people with no
lawyer and no reporter and no way to make

154
00:09:54,341 --> 00:09:55,523
the machinery flinch.

155
00:09:56,123 --> 00:10:00,793
An account emptied in silence, with no
front page to save it, could just as

156
00:10:00,793 --> 00:10:01,789
easily be yours.

157
00:10:02,389 --> 00:10:04,525
And a policy memo is not a law.

158
00:10:05,125 --> 00:10:10,446
It is an instruction the agency writes to
itself — one it can read narrowly, apply

159
00:10:10,446 --> 00:10:14,274
unevenly, or set aside when it decides a
case is different.

160
00:10:14,874 --> 00:10:19,518
Nobody outside the agency voted on it.
Nobody could enforce it.

161
00:10:20,118 --> 00:10:23,694
And for a while, seizures like hers kept
happening anyway.

162
00:10:24,294 --> 00:10:29,731
Which raises the question the front page
did not answer: if the memo really changed

163
00:10:29,731 --> 00:10:30,517
things, why?

164
00:10:33,017 --> 00:10:37,104
You would think that is where it ends. New
policy, money returned.

165
00:10:37,704 --> 00:10:42,439
But a memo is only words on a page, and
words on a page do not always reach a case

166
00:10:42,439 --> 00:10:46,481
already sitting open in a field office in
another part of the country.

167
00:10:47,081 --> 00:10:52,144
In Fairmont, North Carolina, a man named
Lyndon McLellan ran a country convenience

168
00:10:52,144 --> 00:10:52,514
store.

169
00:10:53,114 --> 00:10:57,720
The government seized one hundred seven
thousand, seven hundred two dollars and

170
00:10:57,720 --> 00:11:02,151
sixty-six cents from him — his entire
business account, over the same theory

171
00:11:02,151 --> 00:11:03,842
that had been used on Carole.

172
00:11:04,442 --> 00:11:08,966
And this is the part that should have been
impossible: even after the policy that

173
00:11:08,966 --> 00:11:13,490
was supposed to stop exactly this, the
government pressed ahead, refusing to hand

174
00:11:13,490 --> 00:11:17,120
the money back long after the memo said
cases like his were over.

175
00:11:17,720 --> 00:11:22,539
The seizure had come first, and the
agency's own change of heart never reached

176
00:11:22,539 --> 00:11:24,454
it. Three hundred one deposits.

177
00:11:25,054 --> 00:11:30,248
Around two million dollars in legitimate
sales, rung up over three years, every one

178
00:11:30,248 --> 00:11:32,438
of them under ten thousand dollars.

179
00:11:33,038 --> 00:11:38,220
His store was the kind of place that runs
on small transactions — gas, cigarettes,

180
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:43,465
lottery tickets, a few dollars at a time —
which meant his deposits were naturally,

181
00:11:43,465 --> 00:11:44,602
unavoidably small.

182
00:11:45,202 --> 00:11:49,251
The very shape of an ordinary rural
business was what the government read as a

183
00:11:49,251 --> 00:11:49,614
scheme.

184
00:11:50,214 --> 00:11:54,533
To the government, that pattern was the
case, exactly as it had been in Iowa.

185
00:11:55,133 --> 00:11:58,802
The memo, in other words, had undone
nothing already in motion.

186
00:11:59,402 --> 00:12:04,546
The money was still gone — now simply
held, in a new state, over a pattern that

187
00:12:04,546 --> 00:12:05,393
broke no law.

188
00:12:05,993 --> 00:12:08,083
A difference here would come to sting.

189
00:12:08,683 --> 00:12:13,894
Two people, McLellan and Carole, caught by
the same law, making the same argument,

190
00:12:13,894 --> 00:12:17,135
that the money was clean and the seizure
was wrong.

191
00:12:17,735 --> 00:12:20,846
You would expect the law to treat them the
same way in the end.

192
00:12:21,446 --> 00:12:25,533
It does not, and the reason it does not
will land in a few minutes.

193
00:12:26,133 --> 00:12:30,313
For Carole, by this point, the fight had
already taken its price.

194
00:12:30,913 --> 00:12:35,651
According to her lawyers and the reporting
on her case, while her money was tied up

195
00:12:35,651 --> 00:12:36,393
she sold Mrs.

196
00:12:36,993 --> 00:12:40,383
Lady's, the restaurant she had built over
thirty-eight years.

197
00:12:40,983 --> 00:12:45,455
The money, when it finally came back, came
back to a woman who no longer had the

198
00:12:45,455 --> 00:12:47,020
business it had belonged to.

199
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:49,570
You can return a bank balance.

200
00:12:50,170 --> 00:12:54,257
You cannot return the years, or the thing
a person spent them building.

201
00:12:54,857 --> 00:12:57,876
And then came the number this whole case
had been circling.

202
00:12:58,476 --> 00:13:02,453
A federal watchdog inside the Treasury
Department pulled the records on these

203
00:13:02,453 --> 00:13:03,538
structuring seizures.

204
00:13:04,138 --> 00:13:08,753
In a sample of two hundred seventy-eight
cases where investigators could actually

205
00:13:08,753 --> 00:13:13,026
trace where the money came from,
ninety-one percent of those cases involved

206
00:13:13,026 --> 00:13:15,191
money from legal sources. Nine in ten.

207
00:13:15,791 --> 00:13:18,810
Ordinary businesses, like hers, like
McLellan's.

208
00:13:19,410 --> 00:13:23,609
Across more than two hundred of those
cases, the government had taken in around

209
00:13:23,609 --> 00:13:27,862
seventeen million dollars from people
whose money it could not tie to any crime.

210
00:13:28,462 --> 00:13:31,481
That number changes what all of this is
really about.

211
00:13:32,081 --> 00:13:36,118
If nine of every ten owners caught in this
net turned out to be running clean

212
00:13:36,118 --> 00:13:40,208
businesses, then the seizures were not
catching criminals who slipped through.

213
00:13:40,808 --> 00:13:45,262
The system was working as designed —
flagging the pattern, taking the money,

214
00:13:45,262 --> 00:13:48,192
and waiting to see who could afford to
fight back.

215
00:13:48,792 --> 00:13:53,018
Carole was not an unlucky exception. She
was a representative case.

216
00:13:55,518 --> 00:13:57,283
Remember without prejudice?

217
00:13:57,883 --> 00:14:00,298
Here is what those two words cost her.

218
00:14:00,898 --> 00:14:05,446
Because the government had dismissed her
case that way, walking off the field

219
00:14:05,446 --> 00:14:09,935
instead of admitting it lost, a federal
appeals court ruled, in two thousand

220
00:14:09,935 --> 00:14:13,065
sixteen, that Carole had not substantially
prevailed.

221
00:14:13,665 --> 00:14:18,965
In plain terms, it means this: she got her
money back, but she was not entitled to

222
00:14:18,965 --> 00:14:19,934
her legal fees.

223
00:14:20,534 --> 00:14:25,047
She had been forced to spend to defend
savings that were hers the entire time,

224
00:14:25,047 --> 00:14:27,361
and now she would swallow that cost too.

225
00:14:27,961 --> 00:14:30,608
She won, and still paid to win.

226
00:14:31,208 --> 00:14:33,530
McLellan's case ended the other way.

227
00:14:34,130 --> 00:14:38,888
His money was returned, and a judge
ordered the government to pay his legal

228
00:14:38,888 --> 00:14:41,235
fees, his costs, and interest on top.

229
00:14:41,835 --> 00:14:47,005
The justice Carole never got, he did, the
same law, the same innocence, opposite

230
00:14:47,005 --> 00:14:52,111
endings, decided almost entirely by the
procedural wording of how each case was

231
00:14:52,111 --> 00:14:52,563
closed.

232
00:14:53,163 --> 00:14:57,771
His money came back in the summer of two
thousand fifteen, and the ruling in his

233
00:14:57,771 --> 00:15:02,033
favor read almost like an apology the
government would never say out loud.

234
00:15:02,633 --> 00:15:05,791
And this is not ancient history that got
tidied away.

235
00:15:06,391 --> 00:15:10,682
The power that reached into her account is
still on the books, and the real question

236
00:15:10,682 --> 00:15:13,032
it leaves you with is who it reaches for
next.

237
00:15:13,632 --> 00:15:17,347
By two thousand fifteen, the pressure had
moved to Congress.

238
00:15:17,947 --> 00:15:21,865
Lawmakers held hearings on these
structuring seizures, and business owners

239
00:15:21,865 --> 00:15:25,941
who had lived through them came and
testified about what it had done to their

240
00:15:25,941 --> 00:15:28,535
companies and their families. The stories
landed.

241
00:15:29,135 --> 00:15:33,768
It is one thing to read a policy memo; it
is another to sit in a hearing room and

242
00:15:33,768 --> 00:15:38,515
listen to ordinary people describe having
their accounts emptied over deposits that

243
00:15:38,515 --> 00:15:39,259
broke no law.

244
00:15:39,859 --> 00:15:43,435
And the pressure did not fade when the
headlines did. It built.

245
00:15:44,035 --> 00:15:49,132
Before the reform, an owner could lose
every dollar of working capital and simply

246
00:15:49,132 --> 00:15:52,719
wait, sometimes a year or more, while the
case ground on.

247
00:15:53,319 --> 00:15:56,477
The reform put a clock on the government
for the first time.

248
00:15:57,077 --> 00:15:59,910
In two thousand nineteen, it became law.

249
00:16:00,510 --> 00:16:04,225
A reform act, signed that July, made the
change permanent.

250
00:16:04,825 --> 00:16:09,241
From then on, the government could no
longer take a legal-source account just

251
00:16:09,241 --> 00:16:13,830
because the deposits happened to stay
under ten thousand dollars, and if it does

252
00:16:13,830 --> 00:16:17,271
seize an account, the owner now gets a
hearing within thirty

253
00:16:17,871 --> 00:16:20,565
days, instead of being left to twist for a
year.

254
00:16:21,165 --> 00:16:25,059
It grew out of the fights of business
owners who had lived through their own

255
00:16:25,059 --> 00:16:29,106
seizures — people who turned the worst
year of their lives into a wall standing

256
00:16:29,106 --> 00:16:30,592
between that power and yours.

257
00:16:31,192 --> 00:16:36,219
As for Carole: by the time the law
changed, the business was long gone — she

258
00:16:36,219 --> 00:16:39,923
had sold it, her lawyers say, while the
case dragged on.

259
00:16:40,523 --> 00:16:44,785
Her case, the reporting makes clear, had
become one of the stories that put this

260
00:16:44,785 --> 00:16:47,396
abuse in front of the country in the first
place.

261
00:16:47,996 --> 00:16:52,176
She did not get her fees back, and she did
not get the business back.

262
00:16:52,776 --> 00:16:57,259
What she got is harder to put on a balance
sheet — the knowledge that what happened

263
00:16:57,259 --> 00:17:01,634
to her had not stayed hidden, and that
somewhere in the language of a new federal

264
00:17:01,634 --> 00:17:02,714
law was a door being

265
00:17:03,314 --> 00:17:06,611
shut so it could not happen to the next
person the same way.

266
00:17:07,211 --> 00:17:09,069
And that is the uneasy part.

267
00:17:09,669 --> 00:17:14,437
The reform that grew out of cases like
hers protects you from the exact trap she

268
00:17:14,437 --> 00:17:16,821
fell into, and from almost nothing else.

269
00:17:17,421 --> 00:17:22,478
That same power — to seize first and force
you to fight, at your own cost, to get

270
00:17:22,478 --> 00:17:27,097
your money back — is still on the books,
still working, still reaching for

271
00:17:27,097 --> 00:17:28,845
someone's account right now.

272
00:17:29,445 --> 00:17:33,950
One day, on a slow Tuesday you never saw
coming, it could be reaching for yours.

273
00:17:34,550 --> 00:17:37,522
But the honest ending matters more than
the triumphant one.

274
00:17:38,122 --> 00:17:41,791
That reform closed one door; it did not
close the hallway.

275
00:17:42,391 --> 00:17:47,613
The specific trick that trapped Carole —
treating small, legal deposits as a crime

276
00:17:47,613 --> 00:17:49,078
— is off the table now.

277
00:17:49,678 --> 00:17:54,991
But civil forfeiture itself, the power to
put your property on trial instead of you,

278
00:17:54,991 --> 00:18:00,178
is still very much alive, in drug cases
and highway stops and tax files across the

279
00:18:00,178 --> 00:18:00,684
country.

280
00:18:01,284 --> 00:18:04,024
The law that took Carole's account was
reformed.

281
00:18:04,624 --> 00:18:09,500
The idea behind it was not — and it is
still out there tonight, working the same

282
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:13,401
way it worked on her, on someone who has
no idea it has started.

283
00:18:15,901 --> 00:18:20,537
So the next time someone tells you the
rules exist to protect you, the sharper

284
00:18:20,537 --> 00:18:25,351
question is whose rules, and who decides
what your pattern means. Carole followed

285
00:18:25,351 --> 00:18:25,886
the rule.

286
00:18:26,486 --> 00:18:30,951
The government still emptied her account
and locked away a year of her life. She

287
00:18:30,951 --> 00:18:32,012
got the money back.

288
00:18:32,612 --> 00:18:37,038
By her lawyers' account, she had already
sold the restaurant — and she never got

289
00:18:37,038 --> 00:18:37,813
the year back.

290
00:18:38,413 --> 00:18:42,478
If the machinery running quietly behind
your own bank account is worth twenty

291
00:18:42,478 --> 00:18:46,542
honest minutes of your attention, then you
already understand why this series

292
00:18:46,542 --> 00:18:46,912
exists.

293
00:18:47,512 --> 00:18:52,373
There is one more account this same power
emptied, and that case is the one that

294
00:18:52,373 --> 00:18:54,803
finally moved a courtroom. That is next.

